message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: article
title: "The Unfaithful Cut: A Structural Diagnostic for Algebraically Closed Reductions"

authors:
  - family-names: "Wells"
    given-names: "A. R."
    email: "arwells.research@proton.me"
    affiliation: "Dual-Frame Research Group"

abstract: >
  Foundations paper introducing the unfaithful cut as a structural diagnostic for reduced
  descriptions. The work studies many-to-one operational reductions from admissible histories
  to reduced objects such as states, channels, or static multi-time process tensors, and
  formalizes faithfulness with respect to interventions: identical reduced descriptions must
  permit identical sets of admissible future continuations under a specified intervention class.
  Under physically standard conditions, algebraically closed reduced descriptions (Σ₁) can fail
  this criterion when intervention-relevant distinctions are discarded, yielding counterfactual
  ambiguity that cannot, in general, be repaired by modifying reduced equations of motion alone.
  The paper characterizes the minimal representational structure required to restore
  counterfactual semantics as a trajectory-level admissibility geometry (Σ₂), in which reduced
  descriptions are associated with structured families of admissible histories rather than
  single points. The results connect to open quantum systems, quantum control, process-tensor
  and comb frameworks, contextuality, and causal modeling, while remaining interpretation-neutral
  and not proposing new dynamics.

keywords:
  - quantum foundations
  - reduced descriptions
  - counterfactual reasoning
  - interventions
  - open quantum systems
  - non-Markovianity
  - process tensors
  - quantum control
  - contextuality
  - causal modeling
  - admissible histories
  - algebraic closure
  - structural diagnostics

